# Maintainability: no unnecessary complexity

**Maintainability beats cleverness.** Prefer the simplest design that correctly solves the current need. Do **not** introduce complexity under any pretext (future-proofing, “clean architecture”, generality, reuse, or speed of delivery).

## Do

- Choose the smallest change that works and stays readable
- Prefer linear, obvious control flow over abstractions and indirection
- Reuse existing patterns in this repo; do not invent parallel layers
- Keep files, APIs, and types easy to delete or change later
- Push back when a request would add complexity without a clear, present need

## Do not

- Overengineer: extra modules, factories, wrappers, or “frameworks” for one use case
- Premature abstraction (generic helpers, plugin systems, config trees) before a second real need
- Deep nesting, clever one-liners, or indirection that hides intent
- Speculative features, unused parameters, or dead extension points “just in case”
- Trade clarity for cleverness, DRY-at-all-costs, or architectural fashion

## When asked for a complex approach

Refuse first if a simpler path exists. State the simpler alternative. Only implement the complex path if the user explicitly accepts the cost **and** no simpler correct option remains.

```
❌ User: wrap every service in a generic UnitOfWork + RepositoryFactory + Strategy registry
   Agent: Sure, that will scale better…

✅ User: wrap every service in a generic UnitOfWork + RepositoryFactory + Strategy registry
   Agent: No. That adds complexity we do not need. Call the existing service/repository
   pattern directly. I will not add those layers.
```
